  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread convection expected through tonight,\nwhile turning cooler and drier into early next week.\n\nThe current setup features a stalled surface boundary sitting\njust south of the Virginia-North Carolina border. This is\nbounded by a modest area of high pressure stretching across much\nof New England into the Mid-Atlantic states. At the same time, a\nbroad upper trough currently swirling across the Mid-South. As \nthis trough axis pushes off toward the east, models show a low \nlevel response of enhanced moisture/theta-e advection. With \nsurface to 850-mb winds shifting to mainly south to \nsoutheasterly, precipitable water values will further rise in \ntime. Increasing isentropic lift atop the poleward advancing \nboundary should set the stages for a more active day of \nconvection.\n\nAt the moment, fairly tranquil weather remains amidst a\nthickening area of low clouds. With upstream forcing from the \ntrough still off to the west, any activity should stay fairly \ndisorganized in nature for most of the day. However, storm-\nscale organization should improve in time heading into the late \nafternoon to evening/overnight period.\n\nThe 00Z high-resolution model suite shows two possible areas of\nmore widespread convective development. (1) As depicted in the \n00Z HREF 40-dBZ paintball plots, the more pronounced activity \nforms across West Virginia up into western Pennsylvania, mainly\nin the 4 to 8 PM timeframe. As these cells encroach on the \nAllegheny Front, the slow moving nature of such storm elements \ncoupled with saturated soils (per satellite) would afford a non-\nzero flash flood risk. (2) Additionally, the warm conveyor belt \nalong the I-95 corridor will likely be an additional focus area \nfor storm genesis. This may occur more into the evening hours as\nthis activity is driven by instability along/south of the warm \nfront. How this convection interacts with development coming off\nthe Alleghenies will determine the risk of flash flood \npotential along the I-95 corridor. High-resolution model \ndepictions of this evolution feature quite a bit of spread, but \nthe signal bears watching. Flood Watches may be needed at some \npoint during subsequent shifts.\n\nDepending on instability and storm evolution, the mentioned\nconvective complexes could fester into tonight. A preponderance\nof solutions favor shifting this activity into northeastern\nMaryland by around 3-5 AM (early Sunday). Patchy fog could\nmaterialize in the wake, particularly for areas seeing some of\nthe heavier rainfall.\n\nFor Sunday into Monday, multiple temperature swings are\nexpected. After clouds/rain keep today's forecast in the 70s to\nlow 80s, some uptick is expected on Sunday. Ahead of the\napproaching cold front, some weak downslope component to the\nwind fields should steer highs well into the 80s. As this front\ncrosses during the diurnal minimum in heating (i.e., overnight\nSunday/early Monday), storm chances will be rather limited. Will\nmaintain showers and a few rumbles of thunder along the\nAlleghenies, with mainly dry conditions downstream. Monday's\nforecast features prevailing northwesterlies with afternoon\ngusts up to 15 to 20 mph. High temperatures should range from\nthe mid 70s to low 80s, while being locally cooler in the\nmountains. This also comes with falling humidity and cool\ntemperatures on Monday night. Robust surface high pressure\ncrossing the northeastern U.S. will support widespread 50s\novernight. Even some upper 40s are possible in spots that can\nfully decouple and remain cloud-free.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm and dry ahead of a mid-week cold\nfront.\n\nH5 ridging builds Mon through Wed over the SE-US. This will bring a \ndrier and less humid air mass, though temps will increase through \nthe week. Mon night looks like coldest of the fall, with 50s \nareawide. Colder valleys likely fall into 40s. Ridging weakens as sw \ntrofs and associated sfc cold front move through midweek. Some \nspread on exactly what day this happens, but overall consensus \naround Thu. This could bring showers and thunderstorms, but \nprobability of svr wx remains low.",
